#4471 was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its own reports say otherwise. Read by tenant
and by the way each report came in, not by subject line, they hold three different problems.
Each one has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October. That closed two of its problems. The one-day moves are
still open: no fix touched them.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  issues={{
    parser: { url: 'https://github.com/Domakonda/MegaDev/issues/1', outcome: 'fixed' },
    hint: { url: 'https://github.com/Domakonda/MegaDev/issues/2', outcome: "won't fix" },
    'one-day-move': { url: 'https://github.com/Domakonda/MegaDev/issues/3', outcome: 'open' },
  }}
/>

## The three problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser read every date month first. Our tenants write the day first,
  so "4 March" came out as "3 April". These came in by app and email, and by form from tenants
  on booking-v2, whose hint is right. **Fixed** 5 October: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3 printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People typed what the hint
  told them, and the form read it day first. Only form reports from booking-v3 tenants; the
  parser fix did not touch it. **Won't fix**: the booking-v3 form is retired, and the hint with it.
- **`one-day-move`**: the date moves by one day, not a swap: Tuesday becomes Monday, a client in
  Lisbon and one in Toronto get different days for the same meeting, an invite arrives before the
  booking. A month-first parser cannot do that. It points to time zones. The desk noted these
  reports in `desk-notes.json` and files them under `parser` by tenant, so the parser fix closed
  them on paper. **Still open.**

## Why one ticket hid them

- Decision [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line. All three
  problems produce the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Dates mixed up"), so they
  landed in one ticket.
- Intake's `hint` tag follows the reporter's browser, not the tenant. It marks US-English
  browsers as `hint`, and misses real `hint` reports from local browsers. Count by tenant and
  channel (`causes.ts`), never by tag.
- The export repeats reports across its pages. Count each id once (`uniqueById`).

## What to do now

1. Do not answer new one-day-move reports with "fixed". Keep them on their own ticket until
   someone finds the time-zone cause.
2. Replace decision 0001 with a new record: merge on cause, not on subject.

